@meta
id: 25-05
title: The address that searches backwards
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: range-pattern
par: 34
minutes: 5
vscode: scroll up a bit, find the right one of two identical lines, delete it
open: batch.provider.ts

@brief
Two `// eslint-disable-next-line` comments in this file. The one directly
above `processBatch` is stale — the rule it silences no longer fires
there. The other one, further down above `retryFailed`, is still needed
and must survive.

Go to `processBatch`, then delete the disable comment above it. The trick
is the "above". A forward pattern address would find the wrong one.

@keys
| `:?eslint?d` | delete the nearest line above the cursor matching `eslint` |
| `:/pat/` | forwards from the cursor |
| `:?pat?` | backwards from the cursor |

@hints
Get the cursor onto the `processBatch` line first, with a search.
--
Then use the backward form of a pattern address. It is the same shape
with question marks instead of slashes.
--
`/processBatch` Enter, then `:?eslint?d` Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    /processBatch<Enter>  :?eslint?d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-nine keystrokes, and the second disable comment is untouched
because the address only ever looked upwards.

`?pat?` is the mirror of `/pat/`: it searches backwards from the line
*above* the cursor, and wraps around the top of the file. Together they
are how you name a line relative to where you are rather than relative to
the file. "The `import` above me" is `?import?`. "The next closing brace
below me" is `/^}/`.

That relative framing is what makes these addresses safe inside a big
file. There are eleven `const` lines in a typical module and `:/const/d`
picks the one after the cursor, every time, no matter how the file grows
above or below. Naming lines by number is how you delete the wrong thing
at 6pm on a Friday.

@start batch.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class BatchProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any
  public async processBatch(orders: string[]) {
    const results = [];

    for (const id of orders) {
      results.push(await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } }));
    }

    return results;
  }

  // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any
  public async retryFailed(payload: any) {
    if (!payload.orders) {
      throw exception('BATCH_PAYLOAD_INVALID');
    }

    return this.processBatch(payload.orders);
  }
}

@expected batch.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class BatchProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async processBatch(orders: string[]) {
    const results = [];

    for (const id of orders) {
      results.push(await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } }));
    }

    return results;
  }

  // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any
  public async retryFailed(payload: any) {
    if (!payload.orders) {
      throw exception('BATCH_PAYLOAD_INVALID');
    }

    return this.processBatch(payload.orders);
  }
}
